2. Formal Model and Problem Statement

We model MCSA as a dynamic system of agents, missions, tasks, trust, and memory evolving in discrete time  t = 0,1,2,\dots .

2.1 Agents and Capabilities

Let \mathcal{I} = \mathcal{H} \cup \mathcal{A} be the set of all agents, where \mathcal{H} is the set of human agents and \mathcal{A} the set of AI agents.
Each agent  i \in \mathcal{I}  possesses a capability vector \mathbf{c}_i \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^D, where dimension  d  represents a domain (e.g., mathematics, programming, design, ethical judgement).
For a coalition  C \subseteq \mathcal{I} , its aggregate capability is \mathbf{c}_C = \sum_{i \in C} \mathbf{c}_i.
Agents have limited capacity: an agent can participate in at most  \kappa_i  missions simultaneously. This capacity constraint is essential for realistic resource allocation.

2.2 Missions, Tasks, and Value

A mission  m  is a tuple  m = (T_m, \prec_m, \{\mathbf{r}_\tau\}_{\tau \in T_m}, v_m) , where:

·  T_m  is a finite set of tasks.
·  \prec_m  is a strict partial order on  T_m  (precedence constraints), forming a directed acyclic graph (DAG)  \mathcal{D}_m = (T_m, \prec_m) .
· For each task  \tau \in T_m ,  \mathbf{r}_\tau \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^D  is a requirement vector: the coalition assigned to  \tau  must collectively possess at least  \mathbf{r}_\tau  in every domain for the task to be feasible. (For simplicity we may assume all tasks of a mission share the same requirements; the heterogeneous case is a straightforward extension.)
·  v_m(\mathbf{q}, t_{\text{comp}})  is the value function, where  \mathbf{q} \in [0,1]^{|T_m|}  is the vector of task qualities and  t_{\text{comp}}  is the completion time. Typically  v_m  is non‑decreasing in quality and non‑increasing in time.

A mission is active if it is currently being executed; it is completed once all tasks are finished. The completion time depends on the schedule, which respects precedence constraints and agent capacities.

2.3 Mission Allocation as a Combinatorial Optimisation Problem

At any time  t , a central planner (or a distributed protocol) must assign agents to tasks of active missions subject to:

· Each task  \tau  is assigned a coalition  C_\tau \subseteq \mathcal{I}  with \mathbf{c}_{C_\tau} \ge \mathbf{r}_\tau.
· No agent exceeds its capacity  \kappa_i  across all simultaneous assignments.
· Precedence constraints: a task can start only after all its predecessors have finished.

The objective is to maximise the discounted sum of mission values minus coordination costs (detailed in Section 3). This is an instance of coalition structure generation with precedence and capacity constraints, a known NP‑hard problem (sandholm:1999; rahwan:2009). Therefore, exact optimisation is infeasible for realistic scales.
Our approach is to construct a decentralised, bounded‑rational process that provably converges to a near‑optimal allocation with high probability.

2.4 Mission Hypergraph and Temporal Reachability

The active missions define a hypergraph  \mathcal{H}(t) = (\mathcal{I}, \mathcal{E}(t)) , where each hyperedge  e \in \mathcal{E}(t)  corresponds to a coalition assigned to a specific task of a mission.
Over time, the hypergraph evolves as missions are created, completed, or abandoned.
For the convergence of distributed algorithms, we require a notion of temporal connectivity: the sequence  \{\mathcal{H}(t)\}  is recurrently reachable if for any pair of agents  i,j  there exists a chain of overlapping coalitions such that information can flow from  i  to  j  infinitely often (see casteigts:2012 for formal definitions). This condition ensures that no agent is permanently isolated.

2.5 Trust Tensors

For each ordered pair of agents  i, j  and each domain  d , there is a trust score  T_{i,j}^{(d)}(t) \in [0,1] , representing  i ’s confidence in  j ’s capability in domain  d .
Trust is updated based on observed task outcomes (Section 5).
Trust scores influence coalition formation by modulating the expected probability of task success.

2.6 Collective Memory

The memory is a directed, weighted graph  \mathcal{M}(t) = (K, R(t)) , where  K  is a set of knowledge nodes (solution fragments, facts, lessons) and each edge has a weight  w_{k\ell}(t) \in [0,1]  encoding associative strength.
The memory is used to improve future task execution and to generate opportunity recommendations.

