3. The Civilisation‑Scale Objective

We define the social welfare over a horizon  T  (possibly infinite with discount) as the expected sum of mission values minus coordination costs:

\max_{\text{policies}} \; \mathbb{E}\!\left[ \sum_{m \in \mathcal{M}_{\text{completed}}} v_m(\mathbf{q}_m, t_{\text{comp},m}) - \sum_{t=1}^{T} \gamma^t C(t) \right], \tag{1}

where  C(t)  is the aggregate coordination cost (communication, task switching, etc.) at time  t , and  \gamma \in (0,1]  is a discount factor.
The expectation is over the stochastic execution of tasks, trust evolution, and agent decisions.

Comparison with attention‑centric platforms. Attention‑centric platforms maximise an objective of the form \sum_{t} \gamma^t \sum_{i} f_i(\text{engagement}_{i,t}), where  f_i  is an ad‑specific function. This objective can be recovered from (1) by defining artificial “missions” whose value is linear in engagement time, and ignoring coordination costs. Thus, MCSA is a strict generalisation; it can be calibrated to replicate old metrics, but its natural calibration yields mission‑driven value. Moreover, when missions exhibit positive externalities not captured by individual engagement (e.g., a solved problem benefits many), the two objectives diverge. Under mild conditions, a network optimising (1) will generate higher total welfare than one optimising an engagement proxy; a formal statement of this dominance would require a full game‑theoretic model of both architectures, which we leave for future work.

