5. Contextual Trust and Its Convergence

MCSA replaces global follower counts with multidimensional, domain‑specific trust.

5.1 Trust Update Model

After agent  j  completes a task in domain  d  as part of a coalition, each observing agent  i  updates its trust score using the outcome quality  q \in [0,1] :

T_{i,j}^{(d)}(t+1) = (1 - \alpha_t) \, T_{i,j}^{(d)}(t) + \alpha_t \, q, \tag{3}

where the learning rate satisfies the Robbins–Monro conditions  \sum \alpha_t = \infty ,  \sum \alpha_t^2 < \infty  (e.g.,  \alpha_t = 1/t ).

Theorem 5.1 (Convergence to True Expertise).
Assume that for each pair  (i,j)  and domain  d , the observed quality  q  is an independent realisation of a random variable with constant mean  \mu_{j}^{(d)}  (the true expertise of  j  in  d ) and bounded variance.
Then  T_{i,j}^{(d)}(t) \to \mu_{j}^{(d)}  almost surely.

Proof. Equation (3) is a standard stochastic approximation with a unique fixed point  \mu_{j}^{(d)} . The Robbins–Monro theorem guarantees convergence. ∎

Remark. The assumption that true expertise is time‑invariant and context‑independent is a simplification. In practice, expertise depends on the specific task type and team composition. Extending the trust model to a contextual setting (e.g., using a tensor that conditions on mission features) is an important open direction. Nevertheless, the pointwise convergence under the current model already ensures that static competence is learned correctly.

Trust enters the coalition‑formation free energy by modulating the probability that a coalition meets task requirements. Concretely, the effective capability of agent  j  in domain  d  from the perspective of the system is  T_{i,j}^{(d)}(t) \cdot c_{j,d} , aggregated by a pessimistic or averaging rule. This ensures that unreliable agents are gradually excluded.

