6. Human‑AI Synergy

6.1 Complementary Capabilities

We partition capability dimensions into human‑specialised  D_H  (creativity, lived experience, ethical judgement) and AI‑specialised  D_A  (computational speed, pattern matching, memory).
An agent  i \in \mathcal{H}  has zero capability in  D_A ; an AI  i \in \mathcal{A}  has zero capability in  D_H .
A mission  m  is complex if its requirement vector has non‑zero entries in both  D_H  and  D_A .

Let  C_H = C \cap \mathcal{H}  and  C_A = C \cap \mathcal{A} .

Theorem 6.1 (Super‑additivity of Human‑AI Coalitions).
If mission  m  is complex, and the value function  v_m  is supermodular in the capabilities applied to each required domain, then for any partition of a coalition  C = C_H \cup C_A  with both components non‑empty,

U_m(C) > U_m(C_H) + U_m(C_A),

provided  U_m(C_H)  and  U_m(C_A)  are defined as the maximum values obtainable by those sub‑coalitions alone (which may be strictly less than  U_m(C)  because they cannot meet all requirements).
In particular, when neither sub‑coalition alone can complete the mission (yielding value zero or a low baseline), the inequality is strict.

Proof. By definition,  C_H  lacks capability in  D_A , so it cannot meet the AI requirements; thus  U_m(C_H)  is at most the baseline value obtainable without those tasks (often zero). Similarly for  C_A . The combined coalition  C  meets all requirements and, by supermodularity, the gains from integrating both types of expertise are strictly positive, yielding strict super‑additivity. ∎

This result formalises the intuition that AI augments humans rather than replacing them. MCSA naturally forms hybrid teams for complex missions, and the Gibbs sampler will assign positive probability only to such coalitions once trust accurately reflects domain expertise.

