7. Collective Memory and Knowledge Graph Dynamics

7.1 Hebbian Learning over Knowledge Nodes

The memory graph  \mathcal{M}(t) = (K, R(t))  is updated each time a solution concept  k  is used successfully in conjunction with concept  \ell :

w_{k\ell}(t+1) = (1-\lambda) w_{k\ell}(t) + \eta \cdot \mathbf{1}_{\{k,\ell \text{ co‑occur}\}}, \tag{4}

where  \lambda, \eta \in (0,1)  control decay and reinforcement. This is a form of Hebbian learning.
Under mild conditions (e.g., the co‑occurrence probabilities are stationary), the weight matrix converges to a matrix proportional to the expected co‑occurrence frequencies. This convergence can be analysed with standard stochastic approximation or Markov chain theory; we omit a formal statement due to space.

The memory is queried during task execution to suggest relevant prior solutions, thereby improving task quality and reducing completion time.

7.2 Opportunity Recommendation

Given a user  i  with a history of skills and mission participation, the system recommends new missions (opportunities) to maximise long‑term contribution to social welfare. This is a contextual bandit problem where the reward is the expected marginal value user  i  would bring to a mission.
Using the Gittins index policy (gittins:1979) or Upper Confidence Bound algorithms, the optimal recommendation rule takes the form:

\text{Recommend mission } m \text{ if } \hat{v}_{i,m} + \text{exploration bonus} \ge \tau(T-t),

where  \tau  is a decreasing function of the remaining horizon. This guarantees optimal exploration–exploitation trade‑off.

Theorem 7.1 (Threshold Policy). Under Gaussian priors and independent rewards, the optimal policy is a threshold rule on the posterior mean. The threshold  \tau(T-t)  is monotone decreasing in the remaining time.

Proof. This follows directly from the Gittins index decomposition for independent bandits. ∎

Thus, MCSA optimally surfaces high‑impact missions to the right agents.

