8. Asynchronous Multiplanetary Coordination

When missions span planets with communication delays, task scheduling must be robust.

8.1 Problem Setup

A mission DAG  \mathcal{D}_m  is partitioned among agents on different planets. Communication delay between planets  p  and  q  is  \Delta_{pq} . Agents execute tasks asynchronously, exchanging messages only when necessary.
We define the critical path under ideal synchronous execution,  T_{\text{sync}}^* . The asynchronous execution time  T_{\text{async}}  satisfies

T_{\text{sync}}^* \le T_{\text{async}} \le T_{\text{sync}}^* + \delta,

where  \delta  depends on the maximum delay and the slack in the DAG.

Theorem 8.1 (Asynchronous Coordination Bound).
If the task DAG has slack at least  \Delta_{\max}  on every non‑critical path, then a simple ASAP (as‑soon‑as‑possible) scheduling policy that respects timestamps guarantees  T_{\text{async}} = T_{\text{sync}}^* , i.e., zero overhead.

Proof sketch. Standard results in distributed scheduling with communication delays (e.g., sinnen:2006) show that if the slack absorbs the maximum communication delay, the critical path is unchanged. ∎

For general DAGs, the overhead is bounded by  \Delta_{\max}  times the number of dependency edges that cross planets.

