9. Incentive Compatibility and Mechanism Design

MCSA must incentivise truthful reporting of capabilities, effort, and outcomes.

9.1 The Crowdsourcing Mechanism

Consider a mission  m  with value  V_m  to be shared among participants. Agents report their capability vectors  \hat{\mathbf{c}}_i  and are assigned to tasks. A mechanism computes task allocation and payments. We desire:

· Efficiency (total welfare maximised),
· Incentive compatibility (truthful reporting is a dominant strategy),
· Individual rationality (non‑negative utility),
· Budget balance (sum of payments ≈ 0).

Theorem 9.1 (Impossibility). No mechanism can simultaneously satisfy efficiency, IC, IR, and exact budget balance for all possible agent preferences.

Proof. This follows from the Myerson–Satterthwaite theorem and its extensions to multi‑agent allocation. ∎

We therefore adopt a Vickrey–Clarke–Groves (VCG) mechanism with a redistribution function to achieve approximate budget balance. For large populations, the per‑agent deficit can be made  O(1/\sqrt{|\mathcal{I}|})  under mild symmetry conditions (cavallo:2006). This mechanism also provides resilience against sybil attacks and collapse (strategic withdrawal) through appropriate payment rules, as shown by nath:2012. The exact instantiation for MCSA requires defining agent valuations from mission outcomes, which we leave to future work.

