10. Network Formation: Emergence of Scale‑Free Topology

We show that MCSA naturally grows a power‑law collaboration network.

Model. New agents arrive over time. Upon arrival, an agent  i  selects an existing mission to join, or initiates a new one. The probability that  i  joins mission  m  is proportional to the expected utility  u_i(m) . Assuming utility increases with the size of the existing team (more participants → higher chance of success → higher expected payoff), we obtain a preferential attachment process where the attachment kernel is proportional to the current degree (mission participation count).

Theorem 10.1 (Power‑Law Degree Distribution).
Under the above process with a linear utility‑to‑degree relationship, the steady‑state distribution of agent degrees (number of missions co‑participated) follows  P(k) \propto k^{-\gamma}  with  \gamma = 3 .

Proof. This is the classic Barabási–Albert preferential attachment model (barabasi:1999). The linear kernel is consistent with mission utility scaling linearly with team size in the early growth phase. ∎

Thus, MCSA networks self‑organise into scale‑free collaboration graphs, a signature of real‑world social and technological systems.

