Appendix A. Formal Problem Definitions and Complexity

A.1 Formal Definition of the Mission Allocation Problem

We define the Mission Allocation Problem (MAP) as an integer program.

Instance:

· Set of agents \mathcal{I} = \mathcal{H} \cup \mathcal{A}, each i with capability vector \mathbf{c}_i \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^D and capacity \kappa_i \in \mathbb{N}.
· Set of missions \mathcal{M}, each mission m with:
  · Task DAG \mathcal{D}_m = (T_m, \prec_m).
  · Requirement vectors \mathbf{r}_\tau \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^D for each \tau \in T_m.
  · Value function v_m(\mathbf{q}, t_{\text{comp}}).
· Planning horizon T.

Decision Variables:
For each mission m, task \tau \in T_m, agent i \in \mathcal{I}, and time step t = 0,\dots,T, a binary variable x_{i,\tau}^{m}(t) \in \{0,1\} indicating whether agent i is assigned to task \tau at time t. Tasks require continuous service for a known duration d_\tau, so that an assignment must be a contiguous block.

Constraints:

1. Feasibility: For each task \tau, the collective capability of assigned agents must satisfy \sum_{i} x_{i,\tau}^{m}(t) \cdot c_{i,d} \ge r_{\tau,d} for all d, for each time t during execution.
2. Capacity: For each agent i, at any time t, \sum_{m,\tau} x_{i,\tau}^{m}(t) \le \kappa_i.
3. Precedence: For any edge (\tau_1 \prec \tau_2), the start time of \tau_2 must be at least the completion time of \tau_1.
4. Task integrity: Each task \tau must be executed exactly once (or a given number of times), and the assigned coalition must remain constant for its duration.

Objective: Maximise expected sum of mission values minus coordination costs (as in Eq. (1)).

Theorem A.1 (NP‑hardness).
The Mission Allocation Problem is strongly NP‑hard.

Proof sketch. We reduce from the 3‑Partition problem. Given a set of numbers a_1,\dots,a_{3k} summing to k B and with each a_i \in (B/4, B/2), we construct a single mission with k identical tasks, each requiring a total capability B in a single domain. Agents correspond to numbers a_i, each with capacity 1. There are no precedence constraints, and the value function rewards completion of all tasks. A feasible allocation corresponds exactly to a partition of the numbers into k triples summing to B. Hence MAP is NP‑hard in the strong sense. ∎

This result justifies the use of heuristic and distributed approximation methods.

