Appendix B. Coalition Formation – Full Analysis

B.1 Admissible Coalition Structures

Let the set of all feasible assignments (satisfying capacity, capability, and precedence constraints) be denoted by \mathcal{S}. This is a finite set. We consider only maximal coalition structures where all active missions are assigned some agent team; sub‑optimal structures can be discarded by the optimisation process.

B.2 Block‑Gibbs Sampler

For a given inverse temperature \beta > 0, define the Gibbs distribution over \mathcal{S}:

\pi_\beta(S) \propto \exp\left(-\beta \sum_{m\in\mathcal{M}} F_m(C_m(S))\right),

where C_m(S) is the coalition assigned to mission m in structure S, and F_m(C) = -U_m(C) + \frac{1}{\beta}\log|C|.

We implement a Markov chain on \mathcal{S} that, at rate 1, picks a mission m uniformly at random, and resamples the coalition for m from the conditional distribution \pi_\beta(\cdot \mid S_{-m}), i.e., holding fixed the assignments of all other missions. Because the assignments of different missions are coupled only through agent capacities, this conditional distribution is a Gibbs distribution on coalitions that respects the remaining capacities. The resulting transition kernel K_\beta is aperiodic and, under the recurrent reachability condition, irreducible.

Lemma B.1 (Irreducibility under recurrent reachability).
If the temporal hypergraph of interactions is recurrently reachable (see definition below), then for any fixed \beta, the block‑Gibbs chain is irreducible on the set of all coalition structures that satisfy capacity constraints.

Proof sketch. Recurrent reachability implies that for any two agents i,j there exists a sequence of missions and tasks where they can temporarily share a coalition or pass information. This property allows local changes (swapping agents between missions) to eventually transform any feasible structure into any other through a series of local resampling steps, as long as the capacity constraints can be temporarily relaxed via intermediate states. A formal proof can be constructed by showing that the graph of feasible structures is connected under local moves. ∎

The definition of recurrently reachable we adopt is: for any pair of agents i,j, there exists an infinite sequence of time steps t_1 < t_2 < \dots such that at each t_k, there is a path in the hypergraph \mathcal{H}(t_k) (i.e., a sequence of overlapping hyperedges) connecting i to j. This ensures that information about the global state can propagate to all agents periodically.

B.3 Simulated Annealing

We set \beta(t) = \beta_0 \ln t with \beta_0 > \Delta, where \Delta is the maximum depth of a local minimum of the objective E(S) = \sum_m F_m(C_m(S)). The depth of a local minimum S_0 is the minimum energy barrier E(S') - E(S_0) that must be overcome to reach a state with strictly lower energy. Because \mathcal{S} is finite, \Delta is finite.

Proof of Theorem 4.2 (convergence).
By Hajek’s (1988) theorem on simulated annealing for finite state spaces, if we run a Markov chain X_t with transition kernel K_{\beta(t)} and \beta(t) grows as \beta_0 \ln t with \beta_0 \ge 1/\Delta (or \beta_0 > \Delta depending on the exact formulation), then \lim_{t\to\infty} P(X_t \in \mathcal{S}^*) = 1, where \mathcal{S}^* is the set of global minima of E. Our block‑Gibbs kernel satisfies the conditions of Hajek’s result provided it is irreducible and weak reversible. The recurrent reachability condition ensures irreducibility, and the detailed balance of the Gibbs sampler ensures weak reversibility. Therefore the process converges almost surely to a global minimum, which corresponds to a coalition structure that maximises total utility minus coordination cost. Because the utility U_m incorporates only approximate scheduling, the limit is a near‑optimal allocation up to the approximation error. ∎

Remark. In practice, the block‑Gibbs step can be implemented via distributed message passing: agents broadcast their availability and receive mission “offers” drawn from a soft‑max over estimated values, gradually sharpening the decision as \beta grows. This is similar to a distributed simulated annealing algorithm.

