Appendix C. Trust Convergence – Detailed Proof

We restate Theorem 5.1 and provide a complete proof.

Theorem 5.1 (Convergence to True Expertise).
Let the trust update be

T_{i,j}^{(d)}(t+1) = (1 - \alpha_t) T_{i,j}^{(d)}(t) + \alpha_t Q_t,

where Q_t is the observed quality of agent j in domain d at time t. Assume:

1. \sum_t \alpha_t = \infty, \sum_t \alpha_t^2 < \infty.
2. \mathbb{E}[Q_t \mid \mathcal{F}_{t-1}] = \mu_{j}^{(d)} (constant true expertise), with \text{Var}(Q_t) \le \sigma^2 < \infty.
3. \alpha_t \in (0,1).

Then \lim_{t\to\infty} T_{i,j}^{(d)}(t) = \mu_{j}^{(d)} almost surely.

Proof. Define the estimation error e_t = T_{i,j}^{(d)}(t) - \mu_{j}^{(d)}. The update yields:

e_{t+1} = (1-\alpha_t) e_t + \alpha_t (Q_t - \mu).

This is a linear stochastic approximation. Let V_t = e_t^2. Using the properties of conditional expectation:

\mathbb{E}[e_{t+1}^2 \mid \mathcal{F}_{t-1}] = (1-\alpha_t)^2 e_t^2 + \alpha_t^2 \mathbb{E}[(Q_t-\mu)^2] + 2(1-\alpha_t)\alpha_t e_t \mathbb{E}[Q_t-\mu] = (1-\alpha_t)^2 e_t^2 + \alpha_t^2 \text{Var}(Q_t).

Since \text{Var}(Q_t) \le \sigma^2, we have

\mathbb{E}[e_{t+1}^2 \mid \mathcal{F}_{t-1}] \le (1 - 2\alpha_t + \alpha_t^2) e_t^2 + \sigma^2 \alpha_t^2 \le e_t^2 - 2\alpha_t e_t^2 + O(\alpha_t^2).

Taking unconditional expectations and summing over t, we apply the Robbins–Monro theorem or standard martingale arguments. More directly, the process satisfies the conditions of the Gladyshev theorem (1965): under \sum \alpha_t = \infty, \sum \alpha_t^2 < \infty, the error tends to zero almost surely. ∎

