Appendix D. Human‑AI Synergy – Non‑trivial Super‑additivity

To avoid the trivial case where individual coalitions obtain zero value, we assume each sub‑coalition can still perform the tasks in its domain and yield some base value.

Definition D.1 (Baseline value). For a mission m and a coalition C, let U_m(C) be the maximum expected value obtainable by executing m with C, even if some tasks are left incomplete. If the coalition lacks capability in domain d required by some tasks, those tasks are omitted, but the remaining tasks (which only need domains where C has capability) can be executed.

Theorem 6.1 (Super‑additivity).
If mission m is complex (requires both human and AI domains), and the value function v_m is supermodular in the set of completed tasks (i.e., the marginal value of completing a task is non‑decreasing in the set of other completed tasks), then for any disjoint C_H \subseteq \mathcal{H}, C_A \subseteq \mathcal{A},

U_m(C_H \cup C_A) \ge U_m(C_H) + U_m(C_A) + \Delta,

with \Delta > 0 provided neither sub‑coalition alone can complete the entire mission.

Proof. Let \mathcal{T}_H be the subset of tasks that require only human domains, \mathcal{T}_A require only AI domains, and \mathcal{T}_{H\cap A} require both. A pure human coalition can execute \mathcal{T}_H and possibly \mathcal{T}_{H\cap A} partially (if the AI requirement can be approximated or the task has alternative human‑only methods) – but by assumption they cannot complete all tasks. The same holds for AI. The combined coalition can execute all tasks. By supermodularity, completing the missing cross‑domain tasks adds more value than the sum of the partial values, yielding a strict inequality. ∎

Thus, the synergy is formalised via complementary skills and supermodular rewards.

