Appendix E. Opportunity Recommendation – Gittins Index Threshold

Proof of Theorem 7.1 (Threshold Policy).
We model the recommendation process as a multi‑armed bandit where each mission m corresponds to an arm with unknown reward v_{i,m} (agent i's marginal contribution). At each step, the system selects which mission to recommend. The state is the agent’s posterior belief about each reward. Under independent conjugate priors (e.g., Gaussian), the Gittins index theorem states that the optimal policy is to recommend the mission with the highest dynamic allocation index \nu_m(x), which depends only on the current belief for that arm. For Gaussian arms with known variance, the index is a threshold on the posterior mean: \nu_m(\hat{\mu}_{i,m}) = \hat{\mu}_{i,m} + \sigma \cdot g(\text{remaining horizon}), where g is a decreasing function. The policy is to recommend if the index exceeds a reservation value. Equivalently, we can formulate it as a threshold \tau(T-t) on the posterior mean. This threshold decreases as the horizon shortens because exploration becomes less valuable. The monotonicity follows from the properties of the Gittins index. ∎

For a full derivation, see Gittins (1979) or a standard reinforcement learning text.

