Appendix F. Asynchronous Coordination – Scheduling with Communication Delays

Proof of Theorem 8.1 (Zero Overhead with Slack).
Consider the mission’s DAG \mathcal{D}_m. The critical path CP is the longest path from any source to any sink assuming zero delays. Under ASAP scheduling, each task starts at its earliest possible time given the arrival of all predecessor messages and the availability of agents. If a task \tau on a non‑critical path is delayed because its remote predecessor’s message arrives late, the slack is the amount by which the start of \tau could be delayed without affecting the start of any successor on the critical path. The condition “slack \ge \Delta_{\max}” guarantees that even the maximum communication delay is absorbed by the slack; thus, no task on the critical path is ever delayed. Therefore, the overall completion time equals the synchronous critical path length. A formal proof can be built using the concept of latest start times in the presence of communication delays, as in the literature on DAG scheduling for heterogeneous systems (e.g., Sinnen, 2007). ∎

