Appendix G. Mechanism Design – VCG Redistribution

We outline the Vickrey‑Clarke‑Groves (VCG) mechanism for mission participation. Each agent i reports a valuation function b_i (how much they value participating in a mission, or their cost). The mechanism selects an allocation a^* that maximises the sum of declared valuations. Agent i receives a payment:

p_i = \sum_{j \neq i} b_j(a^*) - \max_{a} \sum_{j \neq i} b_j(a).

This payment internalises the externality. The mechanism is incentive compatible. However, the sum of payments is not balanced; there is a surplus. Redistribution methods (Cavallo, 2006) return a portion of this surplus to participants in a way that preserves incentive compatibility. Under a symmetry assumption (e.g., i.i.d. valuations), the expected budget imbalance per agent is O(1/\sqrt{n}). The exact redistribution rule can be derived from the Cavallo formula: each agent receives an equal share of the surplus that would be generated if the agent’s own valuation were zero. This yields approximate budget balance. The framework also inherits sybil‑proofness properties from Nath et al. (2012), as the payment rule is based on the marginal contribution to the entire coalition, not on local links.

