Appendix H. Network Formation – Preferential Attachment Derivation

We derive the power‑law degree distribution from the proposed growth process.

Model:

· The network starts with a small set of missions and agents.
· At each time step, a new agent i arrives. With probability p, the agent creates a new mission; with probability 1-p, the agent joins an existing mission m with probability proportional to the expected utility u_i(m).

We assume that u_i(m) \propto |C_m| – the size of the existing team on mission m. This is plausible because larger teams tend to have higher success probability and thus higher expected payoff, at least in the early growth phase where congestion is low. Hence the attachment kernel is linear: \Pi(m) = |C_m| / \sum_{m'} |C_{m'}|. This is exactly the Barabási–Albert model with linear preferential attachment. If missions are mapped to nodes in the bipartite graph, agent degree (number of missions joined) follows the same dynamics. The continuum theory yields P(k) \sim k^{-3} for large k. A rigorous stochastic proof can be given via the master equation or the Yule process approximation. Thus, the collaboration network becomes scale‑free.

