Appendix I. Additional Definitions

Temporal Path and Recurrent Reachability (formally):
A temporal path from agent i to agent j in the sequence of hypergraphs \{\mathcal{H}(t)\} is a sequence of time‑stamped edges (e_1, t_1), (e_2, t_2), \dots, (e_k, t_k) with t_1 < t_2 < \dots < t_k such that i \in e_1, j \in e_k, and for each \ell, e_\ell \cap e_{\ell+1} \neq \emptyset. The network is recurrently reachable if for every pair (i,j), such a path exists for infinitely many starting times. This condition guarantees that any two agents can exchange information an unbounded number of times.

State Space for Gibbs Sampler:
\mathcal{S} = \{ S : S \text{ is a mapping from missions to coalitions satisfying capacity constraints} \}. For missions with tasks, the mapping is to a schedule‑consistent assignment. Because agent capacities are bounded, the number of possible assignments is finite.

